---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import methodology from '../data/methodology.json';
---
<BaseLayout title="Methodology" description="How Tokenmaxx normalizes coding-plan allowances, provider-hidden limits, external task economics, model intelligence, and Pareto leaders.">
  <section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Calculation rules</p><h1>Normalize each denominator, then compare only compatible outcomes.</h1><p class="lede">The market uses dollars, token credits, model calls, work units, energy, fair-use windows, and hidden pools. Tokenmaxx preserves those units, derives quality ratios from external publishers, and uses Pareto frontiers instead of forcing everything into one score.</p></section>
  <article class="prose section compact">
    <h2>Evidence states</h2>
    <div class="definition-grid">{Object.entries(methodology.calculationStatuses).map(([key,value]) => <div class="definition"><dt>{key}</dt><dd>{value}</dd></div>)}</div>

    <h2>Why “provider-hidden” is a result</h2>
    <p>Some vendors publish price, models, relative multipliers, reset windows, and concurrency while withholding the numerical base pool. The correct completed record identifies those known mechanics, lists the missing binding denominator, and excludes the plan from allowance rankings. Replacing that gap with a message-count guess would make the dashboard more complete-looking and less true.</p>

    <h2>Core allowance formulas</h2>
    {Object.entries(methodology.formulas).map(([key,value]) => <><h3>{key}</h3><pre><code>{value}</code></pre></>)}

    <h2>Two quality layers</h2>
    <p><strong>Coding Agent Index economics</strong> use an externally published pass-rate-like composite and API cost per task. That supports API dollars per expected pass because the denominator is a task attempt and the score is interpretable as a percentage. The model, reasoning mode, and agent harness stay attached to every row.</p>
    <p><strong>Model Intelligence Index economics</strong> use the publisher’s 70% cache-read, 20% fresh-input, and 10% output blended token price. Dividing that price by Intelligence Index yields a cost-density signal per million blended tokens per index point. Intelligence Index is not a pass probability, so it is never converted into expected completed tasks.</p>

    <h2>Pareto frontiers</h2>
    <p>A configuration is non-dominated when no other imported configuration is at least as capable and also cheaper, faster, or more generous on the selected axis. Tokenmaxx publishes separate frontiers for coding quality versus API cost, coding quality versus time, coding quality versus raw tokens, model intelligence versus blended price, model intelligence versus speed, monthly access price versus direct model intelligence, and quantified plan allowance versus direct model intelligence.</p>
    <p>The dashboard includes a labeled <strong>quality-adjusted subsidy signal</strong> equal to plan value multiple × Intelligence Index / 100 and its inverse, subscription dollars per intelligence-weighted provider-value dollar. Both are heuristics; neither replaces the original two-axis frontier. Subscription dollars per Intelligence Index point is shown separately as an access-price density that deliberately ignores allowance size.</p>

    <h2>Subscription task estimates</h2>
    <p>A monthly expected-pass calculation is shown only when three facts align: the subscription has a quantified API-equivalent allowance, the external benchmark tested that native agent route, and the benchmark publishes API cost per task. At present the clean join is available for measured Codex plans and native Codex GPT-5.6 configurations. Five-hour windows, shared ChatGPT usage, and workload mix can still bind first.</p>

    <h2>External benchmarks and future community runs</h2>
    <p>The current release imports external results only. The community contribution schema pins benchmark version, task-set hash, harness commit, model route, authentication mode, every attempt, verifier output, and redacted artifacts. Community results will remain a separate evidence lane rather than being silently merged into publisher-owned leaderboards.</p>

    <h2>Principles enforced by the data model</h2><ul>{methodology.principles.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
